  "notes": [
    "Running heads (THE CRISIS, THE CRISIS ADVERTISER, a section name repeated at the top of a page) and page numbers are never articles.",
    "Along the Color Line is a department of short news items grouped under small-caps subheads such as POLITICAL, EDUCATION, THE CHURCH, ECONOMIC, SOCIAL UPLIFT, ORGANIZATIONS AND MEETINGS, SCIENCE, ART, JUDICIAL DECISIONS. Each news paragraph is its own article; the subhead is its section, not an article. Never merge items from two different subheads into one article.",
    "Opinion is a run of quotations from other newspapers and magazines, usually introduced by the source's name (\"The Boston Transcript says:\"). Each quotation is a separate article, with the quoted paper as its source; never make a whole Opinion page one article. Foreign Comment works the same way.",
    "Editorial (by Du Bois) is a series of short editorials, each starting under its own capitalized heading (SEGREGATION., VOTING., THE GHETTO). Each heading starts a new article that runs until the next heading, across a page break if needed.",
    "Letters: each letter is a separate article. A heading such as FROM DUTCH WORKINGMEN. or FROM A SOUTHERN WHITE MAN. starts a letter, and everything after it up to the next heading, including a quoted motion or resolution, belongs to that letter.",
    "Signed feature articles (e.g. THE REAL RACE PROBLEM by Franz Boas) run across several pages; the title repeated as a running head on later pages continues the same article, it does not start a new one."
  ],
